Z||000000||bHooper||12-14-2000||09:31 AM||||last night i installed my KYB shocks and struts, along with Eibach springs.<P>i have a few questions, before i take the car off to the alignment shop.<P>1) i noticed that the KYB shock's are a bit longer than the new Monroe's that I had previously installed... is this typical?<BR>i had to compress them to get them in.<P>2) the KYB struts, they are practically compressible between the palms of my hands.  when i mounted them and lowered the car, the setteled with easy.  is this normal?<P>3) at that moment (of dropping the car on all 4's) i seemed to remember something i had read a long time ago, about turning the strut piston to "lock" it into position.  is that true, or are the KYB's just like any other strut?<P>the gabriels require force to compress.<P>i just wanted to make sure everything is ok, and that the rear springs aren't doing all the supporting work.<P>i also measured the car height, from ground to top of the tire well: front, 26" (with stock 205/60/15 tires), rear, 27 (with 215/65/15 tires)<P>thanks in advance!<P>hoop<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-14-2000||11:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>last night i installed my KYB shocks and struts, along with Eibach springs.<P>i have a few questions, before i take the car off to the alignment shop.<P>1) i noticed that the KYB shock's are a bit longer than the new Monroe's that I had previously installed... is this typical?<BR>i had to compress them to get them in.<P>2) the KYB struts, they are practically compressible between the palms of my hands.  when i mounted them and lowered the car, the setteled with easy.  is this normal?<P>3) at that moment (of dropping the car on all 4's) i seemed to remember something i had read a long time ago, about turning the strut piston to "lock" it into position.  is that true, or are the KYB's just like any other strut?<P>the gabriels require force to compress.<P>i just wanted to make sure everything is ok, and that the rear springs aren't doing all the supporting work.<P>i also measured the car height, from ground to top of the tire well: front, 26" (with stock 205/60/15 tires), rear, 27 (with 215/65/15 tires)<P>thanks in advance!<P>hoop<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>1. My KYB's were the same height as the original strut's. (GM original)<P>2. When I compressed my KYB's by hand they required effort to do. How "easy" was it?<P>3. I thought it was the KONI's that had the "lock" feature since you could adjust how firm you want them. I never saw anything on my KYB's or instructions regarding a "lock" position. I have installed KYB's on a few different cars and they all were basically the same.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||12-14-2000||11:45 AM||||well they do require some effort, just not as much as the gabriels.<P>maybe it was the knoi's i read about... i knew it was one of the two.<P>what about the length of the kyb shocks, i thought that was curious.<P>thanks!<P>hoop||||reg||1
